to-rasmus-mogelberg-source : List Word
to-rasmus-mogelberg-source = lines-of (cons "# Rasmus Ejlers Møgelberg"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Professor of computer science, IT University of Copenhagen. This is written to you by Avik Jain of Hyperactive Labs. It is about what your work is, what it makes possible, and where it sits in a system that rests on it. Each claim below about Avik's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What you built"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field reads guarded recursion as a technique: a modal form of step-indexing, a convenient way to build models of languages with higher-order store, a device that makes certain domain equations solvable. That reading is accurate and it undersells the work."
  (cons ""
  (cons "What the later modality ▷ does is give time a type. A guarded fixed point, fix : (▷A → A) → A, says that a definition may refer to itself only one step later. That one rule moves productivity out of a syntactic guardedness check, which every coinductive proof assistant otherwise patches on from outside, and into the types, where it composes. Clocks then make the delay local: ∀κ turns a guarded stream back into a coinductive one, and several processes run on independent clocks without interfering."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Computation on a machine is mostly of that kind: a server, a protocol, an agent waiting on input, a view kept up to date while its sources change. Type theory grew up around functions that terminate and return. You gave it a native account of the other half."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The cubical step"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper's clocked extension cites one work: yours with Kristensen and Vezzosi"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1, ref. 44)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "With Niccolò Veltri you showed at POPL 2019 that for guarded recursive types, bisimilarity is the path type: two processes that agree on every observation are equal, as a theorem rather than an assumption. Then with Magnus Baunsgaard Kristensen and Andrea Vezzosi, in Greatest HITs (LICS 2022), you combined multi-clocked guarded recursion with cubical type theory and gave the induction principle under clocks. Quantifying over clocks commutes with higher inductive types up to equivalence, with real computation rules, where earlier work had only an axiom. Coinductive types can then be built from quotients: finitely branching labelled transition systems, finite powersets, finite distributions. Bisimilarity implies path equality there too, so any proof about one process transports along a bisimulation to every process that behaves the same."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The consequence is the important part. Once equality of behaviour is equality, proofs about one process carry to every process that behaves the same, with no separate theory of observational equivalence bolted on. That is clocked cubical type theory's real content, and it is still read mostly as a technical union of two research programmes."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your TYPES 2025 paper, Multi-clocked Guarded Recursion Beyond ω, finishes a piece of that foundation. Extending the extensional presheaf model to higher ordinals carries the encodings of finite powersets, finite distributions and existentially defined coinductive predicates past W-types, and lets results proved in Clocked Cubical Type Theory be read in a set-theoretic model."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity's computational rules follow the CCHM calculus, extended with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". In it types and terms are not two species of artifact"
  (cons (cite s2-p9 "(§2)")
  (cons ", and a content-addressed codebase holds programs together with their derivations, equivalences, dependency certificates and proofs"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Your half of the core is what lets that system describe continuing activity rather than only terminating functions."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Continuing processes are guarded terms.** A continuing process returns, takes a silent delayed step, or issues a command with a response-indexed delayed continuation, and `bind` is defined by guarded recursion, never by eliminating an arbitrary later value"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p19 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". For a running native loop, guarded fixed-point induction closes the back edge of the simulation proof, and an ongoing no-resource-failure property `Always^κ` is defined by guarded recursion on successive turns, under the stated allocation and collection premises"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p39 "(§7.6, 7.6e)")
  (cons ". Fairness and environmental responses remain premises, not consequences of guardedness"
  (cons (cite s10-p14 "(§10)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Equal behaviour, and its evidence.** Native continuing execution is related to its source by a two-sided, termination-sensitive guarded relation, the greatest clock-guarded relation over weak observable heads, which distinguishes divergence from deadlock"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p31 "(§7.6, 7.6c–7.6d)")
  (cons ". The store records each relation between definitions with its actual evidence, and keeps address identity, definitional equality and semantic equivalence distinct"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8, 10.22)")
  (cons ". Your POPL 2019 and LICS 2022 results are what make bisimilarity a path in the clocked cubical setting the paper cites, so that a relation of behaviour can be an identification along which proofs transport."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Observation is finite and refines.** What an observation warrants is a construction over the fiber of compatible histories, and refining the observation keeps every conclusion already established"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons (cite s10-4-p6 "(§10.4)")
  (cons ". The paper's debugger works this way on a running program: an observed event prefix selects the fiber of compatible runs, and each new event restricts it further"
  (cons (cite s10-9-p5 "(§10.9, 10.23–10.24)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Measure lives inside the language.** When a probabilistic description is supplied, its probability structure is additional information on which further reasoning operates, not a stand-in for what is missing"
  (cons (cite s1-p14 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Induction under clocks gives coinductive types over finite powersets and finite distributions, which is what such constructions need. Your current turn toward probabilistic programming lands on the same ground."
  (cons ""
  (cons "None of this is your work restated. It is what your work makes possible when it is taken as the foundation of a whole computing system."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is yours, with full credit. That covers guarded recursion and the later modality, multiple clocks, the guarded dependent type theory with coinductive types (with Bizjak, Grathwohl, Clouston and Birkedal), bisimulation as path (with Veltri), induction under clocks and Clocked Cubical Type Theory (with Kristensen and Vezzosi), and the set-theoretic soundness beyond ω. Where your coauthors share the work, they share the credit in the same way. Nothing here is framed as superseding it. The system exists because you did it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In ITU's portrait of you in October 2025 you said the aim is to prove that programs are correct rather than test them. The paper states the same method: a test observes selected executions, whereas the rationale concerns every input in its stated domain"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1, 1.5)")
  (cons ". We would like to show you what your theory carries once it runs underneath everything, and to hear where you see it going next."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- ITU, Professor portrait: Rasmus Ejlers Møgelberg creates new mathematical worlds (October 2025), https://en.itu.dk/About-ITU/Press/News-from-ITU/2025/Professor-Portrait-Rasmus-Ejlers-Moegelberg-creates-new-mathematical-worlds"
  (cons "- Multi-Clocked Guarded Recursion Beyond ω, TYPES 2025, https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11361v1"
  (cons "- Greatest HITs, LICS 2022, https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.01969v2"
  (cons "- Bisimulation as path type for guarded recursive types, POPL 2019, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1810.13261"
  (cons "- Guarded dependent type theory with coinductive types, FoSSaCS 2016, https://www.arxiv.org/abs/1601.01586"
  nil
